WEBVTT

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.675
Performance tuning starts by separating prompt processing speed from token generation

00:00:04.675 --> 00:00:05.360
speed.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.120
Prompt processing is the work of reading the input context.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.955
Token generation is the step-by-step creation of the answer.

00:00:10.955 --> 00:00:14.505
A system may feel fast with short prompts but slow with long documents.

00:00:14.505 --> 00:00:18.605
Measuring both phases helps identify whether the issue is context size, model size,

00:00:18.605 --> 00:00:21.155
runtime settings, or hardware limits.

00:00:21.155 --> 00:00:23.480
Context window size has a direct operational cost.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.960
Larger context can help document analysis and long conversations,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.576
but it increases memory use and processing time.

00:00:29.576 --> 00:00:31.110
Larger models are not always better either.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:36.020
A model that barely fits may swap memory, run slowly, or become unstable under real use.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.755
A slightly smaller model with a sensible context setting may produce a better user

00:00:39.755 --> 00:00:40.595
experience.

00:00:40.595 --> 00:00:43.370
GPU acceleration, batch concepts, thread settings,

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:46.120
and runtime parameters can influence performance,

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.920
but changing too many settings at once makes troubleshooting harder.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.580
Establish a baseline with a known model, known prompt, context length,

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.760
and runtime version.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.250
Then change one variable at a time.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:59.395
Record the effect on load time, prompt processing, generation speed, memory pressure,

00:00:59.395 --> 00:01:00.980
and output quality.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:03.400
Thermal throttling and memory pressure are practical realities.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.325
A laptop under sustained load may reduce performance to manage heat and battery.

00:01:07.325 --> 00:01:10.885
A system under memory pressure may slow down or become unstable.

00:01:10.885 --> 00:01:14.560
Monitor disk, memory, temperature, and service behavior during real workloads.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.695
The best tuning process is measured, repeatable,

00:01:16.695 --> 00:01:19.810
and tied to the task rather than chasing the largest possible numbers.
